.TH "flag" "1" "2026\-11\-16" "flag 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.PP
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
flag \- fast discovery of command flags from man pages
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBflag\fR [\fIflags\fR] [\fIarguments\fR]
.PP
\fBflag\fR [\fIarguments\fR] [\fIflags\fR]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
flag is a command\-line tool for fast discovery of command flags from man pages.\& 
.br
flag relies on the man(1) and cat(1) commands for parts of it'\&s functionality, 
.br
effectively executing "\fBman [\fR\fIarguments\fR\fB] 2>/dev/null | cat\fR" to get the man 
.br
page for the specified command and then matching it against a regular expression 
.br
(regex).\& 
.br
Because flag uses regex for it'\&s functionality, there are some known 
.br
limitations; see \fBLIMITATIONS\fR below.\&
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
\fB--color=\fR\fIWHEN\fR
.RS 4
Use markers to highlight the output; \fIWHEN\fR is \fBnever\fR, \fBauto\fR or \fBalways\fR.\&
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
The default.\& If \fBnever\fR is used, the colors will never be used.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
If \fBauto\fR is used, the colors will be used only if the output is sent to a 
.br
tty.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
If \fBalways\fR is used, the colors will always be used regardless of where 
.br
the output is sent.\&
.PD
.PP
.PP
.RE
\fB-c, \-\-column=\fR\fIN\fR
.RS 4
List entries by columns; \fIN\fR specifies the column count.\& 
.br
If the \fB\-\-column=\fR\fIN\fR flag is set, the \fB\-\-delimiter=\fR\fIDEM\fR value is ignored.\& 
.br
If too many columns are specified or if the flag output is too long to fit 
.br
into the terminal width, the output will overflow (adding the \fB\-\-no\-argument\fR 
.br
flag can be useful here).\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-d, \-\-delimiter=\fR\fIDEM\fR
.RS 4
Specify delimiter \fIDEM\fR used for separating flags; the characters '\&\en'\& 
.br
(the default) and '\&\et'\& retain special meaning, i.\&e.\& the new line and tab 
.br
characters respectively.\& 
.br
If the \fB\-\-column=\fR\fIN\fR flag is set this flag is ignored.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-D, \-\-debug\fR
.RS 4
Show debug messages (this outputs a lot of information about the state and 
.br
behavior of the program).\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-e, \-\-enclose=\fR\fIENC\fR
.RS 4
Specify \fIENC\fR used for enclosing (prefixing and suffixing) the 
.br
output; 0, 1 or 2 characters are expected.\&
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
If 0 characters are given, e.\&g.\& \fB\-e ""\fR, then the output is not enclosed 
.br
(this is the default).\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
If 1 character is given, e.\&g.\& \fB\-e "|"\fR, then it is used for both 
.br
prefixing and suffixing the output.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
If 2 characters are given, e.\&g.\& \fB\-e "[]"\fR, then the first character is used 
.br
for prefixing and the second character is used for suffixing the output.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
If >=3 characters are given then the program prints an error message and 
.br
exits with exit value 1.\&
.PD
.PP
.RE
\fB-h, \-\-help\fR
.RS 4
Show help message.\& The help message is also displayed if the flag 
.br
command is used with no flags or arguments.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-l, \-\-long\fR
.RS 4
Show long flag notation.\& This notation shows only flags starting 
.br
with \fB\-\-\fR and immediately followed by the flag name, e.\&g.\& \fB\-\-help\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-m, \-\-mixed\fR
.RS 4
Show mixed flag notation.\& This notation effectively shows the output of 
.br
the \fB\-s\fR, \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-M\fR flags combined, i.\&e.\& flags that have only the short 
.br
or long notation are shown separately and flags that have both the short and 
.br
long notations are shown together, hence the mixed notation gives the 
.br
largest output, e.\&g.\& \fB\-h|\-\-help\fR.\& This is the default notation used.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-M, \-\-mixed\-only\fR
.RS 4
Show only flags that have both the short and long notations.\& Unlike the \fB\-m\fR 
.br
flag, this flag doesn'\&t show flags that have only the short or long 
.br
notations, hence this flag shows the least amount of output.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-n, \-\-no\-argument\fR
.RS 4
This flag specifies to not include the flag arguments in the output 
.br
(this relates to both short and long flags), e.\&g.\& \fB\-\-enclose\fR \fIENC\fR would be 
.br
shown as \fB\-\-enclose\fR.\& This can greatly reduce the length of the output and 
.br
can be paired nicely with the \fB\-\-column\fR \fICOL\fR flag.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-p, \-\-pretty\fR
.RS 4
Alias for \fB'\&\-\-color=\fR\fIalways\fR \fB\-\-enclose=\fR\fI[]\fR'\&.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-r, \-\-reverse\fR
.RS 4
Reverse the order of the output.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-s, \-\-short\fR
.RS 4
Show short flag notation.\& This notation shows only flags starting 
.br
with \fB\-\fR and immediately followed by the flag name, e.\&g.\& \fB\-h\fR.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-S, \-\-sort\fR
.RS 4
Sort the output lexicographically.\& By default the flags are sorted by the 
.br
order of appearance in the man page.\& The sort is case agnostic, i.\&e.\& a 
.br
lowercase '\&z'\& will not come before an uppercase '\&A'\&, however with equal 
.br
letters the lowercase version takes precedence, e.\&g.\& and '\&a'\& will come 
.br
before an '\&A'\&.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-t, \-\-title\fR
.RS 4
Show the first line from the man page \fBNAME\fR section at the bottom of the 
.br
output.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-v, \-\-version\fR
.RS 4
Display version information and exit.\&
.PP
.RE
\fB-w, \-\-flag\-count\fR
.RS 4
Show the flag count at the bottom of the output.\& This displays 4 different 
.br
values where:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBSF\fR stands for Short Flags.\& If the \fB\-m\fR flag is used this counts the 
.br
flags that have only the short notation.\& If the \fB\-M\fR flag is used this value 
.br
is always 0.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBLF\fR stands for Long Flags.\& If the \fB\-m\fR flag is used this counts the flags 
.br
that have only the long notation.\& If the \fB\-M\fR flag is used this value is 
.br
always 0.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBMF\fR stands for Mixed Flags.\& If the \fB\-m\fR or \fB\-M\fR flags are used, this 
.br
shows the count of flags that have both the short and long notations.\& 
.br
If the \fB\-s\fR or \fB\-l\fR flags are used this values is always 0.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
\fBTT\fR stands for total.\& It displays the total count of flags found 
.br
(flags that have only the short or long notations and flags that have both 
.br
short and long notations).\&
.PD
.PP
.RE
\fB\-\-\fR
.RS 4
Treats all subsequent arguments as file names, even if they start with a 
.br
dash.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
.RS 4
The following exit values should be returned:
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
0  if the program runs successfully.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
>0 if an error occurs or an exceptions is thrown.\&
.PD
.PP
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
.PP
.RS 4
Considering the fact that regex is used for getting the flags from the man 
.br
pages and there isn'\&t a rigorous standard that most of the man pages follow, 
.br
there are certain limitation to the expected output.\&
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Firstly, some man pages list a flag and it'\&s aliases on the same line, 
.br
e.\&g.\& \fB\-\-quiet\fR, \fB\-\-silent\fR in head(1), whilst others list different, 
.br
similar flags on the same line, e.\&g.\& \fB\-4\fR, \fB\-6\fR in traceroute(8).\& 
.br
This induces an ambiguity where it isn'\&t clear whether the flag is an alias 
.br
or a different, similar flag.\& In this case the flag command expects there to 
.br
be at maximum one short and one long flag on the same line, hence any 
.br
additional flags are not matched and don'\&t show up in the output.\& 
.br
This can also cause opposite versions of flags not to be matched if they are 
.br
both defined on the same line, e.\&g.\& \fB\-\-column[=\fR\fI<options>\fR\fB]\fR, \fB\-\-no\-column\fR 
.br
in git\-branch(1).\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Secondly, the flag command follows the "POSIX.\&1\-2017" utility 
.br
conventions, which state that: "All options should be preceded by 
.br
the '\&\-'\& delimiter character".\& This results in the fact that some flags that 
.br
don'\&t start with the '\&\-'\& delimiter are not added to the output, e.\&g.\& 
.br
\fB+FIRST_PAGE[:LAST_PAGE]\fR from pr(1) is ignored.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Thirdly, there are some commands that implicitly assume that a flag has to 
.br
be prefixed with some sort of a character, e.\&g.\& \fB\-Si\fR in pacman(8).\& 
.br
In these cases if the prefix is not stated explicitly for each of the flags 
.br
the context is lost and the flags are added to the output without the 
.br
prefix.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Fourthly, some man pages might contain text in other sections such as 
.br
the \fBEXAMPLE\fR section which can'\&t be explicitly classified as not being a 
.br
flag, e.\&g.\& \fB\-2\fR in the fzf(1) \fBFIELD INDEX EXPRESSION\fR section.\& 
.br
These cases lead to false positives where text is matched as being a flag 
.br
even though it isn'\&t a flag.\&
.PD
.PP
.PD 0
.IP \(bu 4
Fifthly, the empty long flag \fB\-\-\fR, which is often used as a delimiter 
.br
indicating the end of options, is deliberately not matched.\&
.PD
.PP
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 4
\fBflag ls\fR
.RS 4
Show the flags from ls(1).\&
.RE
\fBflag git log\fR
.RS 4
Show the flags from git\-log(1).\&
.RE
\fBflag \-n ls\fR
.RS 4
Show the flags from ls(1) with no arguments.\&
.RE
\fBflag \-\-color=always \-\-column=2 \-\-enclose=[] \-M \-n \-S \-r \-t \-w ls\fR
.RS 4
Show the flags from ls(1) with colored output, printed in 2 
.br
columns, prefixed with '\&['\& and suffixed with '\&]'\&, showing only flags 
.br
that have both the short and long notations, with no arguments, sorted 
.br
in the reverse order with the title and flag count added to the bottom 
.br
of the output.\&
.PP
.RE
.RE
